    <section class="author-note content-width">
      <p class="section-label">About this publication</p>
      <h2>Large liability claims deserve a clear explanation.</h2>
      <p>
        Most people purchase homeowners and auto insurance believing those policies
        will protect them if something goes wrong. In many situations, they will.
        But severe accidents and lawsuits can create damages that exceed the liability
        limits of an underlying policy.
      </p>
      <p>
        This handbook explains what umbrella insurance is designed to do, how excess
        liability differs from personal injury coverage, what common insurance terms
        mean, where coverage may stop, and which questions are worth asking before
        purchasing a policy.
      </p>

      <div class="notice-card">
        <h3>Educational disclaimer</h3>
        <p>
          This guide provides general educational information and is not individualized
          insurance, legal, financial, or claims advice. Policy forms, definitions,
          exclusions, required underlying limits, available coverages, pricing, and
          claims practices vary by insurer and state. The policy contract controls.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

