        <section class="part" id="part-1">
          <p class="part-number">Part I</p>
          <h2>What Umbrella Insurance Is and Why It Exists</h2>

          <h3>What Is Umbrella Insurance?</h3>
          <p>
            Umbrella insurance provides an additional layer of personal liability
            protection beyond the liability limits of qualifying underlying policies,
            such as homeowners, auto, motorcycle, recreational vehicle, or boat
            insurance.
          </p>
          <p>
            It does not replace those policies. It works with them. When a covered
            liability claim exceeds the applicable underlying policy limit, an umbrella
            policy may help pay the remaining covered amount, up to the umbrella limit.
          </p>

          <div class="callout">
            <strong>A simple way to think about it</strong>
            <p>
              Your underlying policy is the first layer. Your umbrella policy is an
              additional layer that may respond after the first layer reaches its
              covered limit.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            For many families, umbrella insurance is a cost-effective way to help
            protect savings, investments, future income, and other personal assets from
            unusually large covered liability claims.
          </p>

          <h3>Why Would Someone Need an Umbrella Policy?</h3>
          <p>
            Lawsuits are not limited to celebrities, corporations, or exceptionally
            wealthy people. Anyone can be sued after an accident or event that causes
            serious injury, significant property damage, or another covered harm.
          </p>
          <p>
            A major automobile accident, an injury at your home, a dog-bite incident,
            a recreational accident, or a covered personal injury allegation can create
            damages greater than the limits carried on a standard policy.
          </p>
          <p>
            If a covered judgment or settlement exceeds the available underlying
            liability limit, you may become personally responsible for the unpaid
            balance. Umbrella insurance is designed to provide another layer of
            protection against that possibility.
          </p>

          <blockquote>
            Umbrella insurance is not purchased because you expect a catastrophic
            claim. It is purchased because one severe event can carry consequences
            far greater than an everyday policy limit.
          </blockquote>

          <a class="next-link" href="#part-2">Continue to Part II &rarr;</a>
        </section>

        <section class="part" id="part-2">
          <p class="part-number">Part II</p>
          <h2>What the Policy May Cover</h2>

          <h3>What Does an Umbrella Policy Typically Cover?</h3>
          <p>
            Every insurer writes its policy differently, but a personal umbrella policy
            may provide additional protection for covered:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Bodily injury liability</li>
            <li>Property damage liability</li>
            <li>Legal defense costs, when covered by the policy</li>
            <li>Certain personal injury offenses</li>
            <li>Liability above qualifying home, auto, motorcycle, boat, or other underlying policies</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            An umbrella policy is liability coverage. It is not designed to repair your
            own vehicle, rebuild your own home, replace your belongings, or pay your own
            medical bills.
          </p>

          <h3>Understanding the Insurance Terms</h3>

          <div class="definition-grid">
            <section class="definition-card">
              <h4>Excess Liability</h4>
              <p>
                Additional liability protection that may begin after the applicable
                underlying liability limit has been exhausted by a covered claim.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="definition-card">
              <h4>Bodily Injury Liability</h4>
              <p>
                Liability for physical injury or death you are legally responsible for
                causing to another person. Covered damages may include medical expenses,
                lost income, rehabilitation, pain and suffering, funeral expenses,
                settlements, or judgments.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="definition-card">
              <h4>Property Damage Liability</h4>
              <p>
                Liability for physical damage you are legally responsible for causing
                to someone else's property, such as a vehicle, home, fence, garage,
                landscaping, boat, dock, or other covered property.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="definition-card">
              <h4>Personal Injury</h4>
              <p>
                In this context, personal injury generally means certain non-physical
                offenses, not bodily injury. Depending on the policy, it may include
                libel, slander, defamation, false arrest, false imprisonment, malicious
                prosecution, wrongful eviction, wrongful entry, or invasion of the
                right of private occupancy.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="definition-card">
              <h4>Libel</h4>
              <p>
                A false written or published statement that harms another person's
                reputation, such as a false social-media post, article, or online review.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="definition-card">
              <h4>Slander</h4>
              <p>
                A false spoken statement that harms another person's reputation.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="definition-card">
              <h4>Defamation</h4>
              <p>
                The broader legal category involving false statements that harm
                reputation. Libel is generally written or published defamation;
                slander is generally spoken defamation.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="definition-card">
              <h4>Emotional Distress</h4>
              <p>
                Some lawsuits allege severe emotional or psychological harm. Coverage
                is not automatic merely because emotional distress is alleged. It
                depends on the facts, the legal allegations, and the policy language.
              </p>
            </section>
          </div>

          <a class="next-link" href="#part-3">Continue to Part III &rarr;</a>
        </section>

        <section class="part" id="part-3">
          <p class="part-number">Part III</p>
          <h2>Excess Liability and Personal Injury Limits</h2>

          <h3>Excess Liability Coverage</h3>
          <p>
            Excess liability is the primary protection provided by an umbrella policy.
            It adds another amount of liability coverage above the qualifying underlying
            policy limit.
          </p>

          <h4>Common Excess Liability Limits</h4>
          <ul class="limit-list">
            <li>$1 million</li>
            <li>$2 million</li>
            <li>$3 million</li>
            <li>$4 million</li>
            <li>$5 million</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Some insurers may offer higher limits, subject to eligibility and
            underwriting.
          </p>

          <div class="scenario">
            <p class="scenario-label">Example: Serious automobile accident</p>
            <h4>How the layers may work</h4>
            <p>
              You are found legally responsible for a covered accident resulting in
              $1.3 million in bodily injury damages. Your auto policy provides a
              $300,000 applicable liability limit.
            </p>
            <ol>
              <li>The auto policy pays up to its covered $300,000 limit.</li>
              <li>The remaining covered damages equal $1 million.</li>
              <li>A $1 million umbrella policy may respond to that remaining amount, subject to all policy terms, conditions, and exclusions.</li>
            </ol>
          </div>

          <div class="scenario">
            <p class="scenario-label">Example: Injury at your home</p>
            <h4>A claim exceeds the homeowners limit</h4>
            <p>
              A guest or contractor suffers a severe covered injury at your property.
              The settlement includes medical expenses, lost income, rehabilitation,
              and pain and suffering. After the homeowners policy reaches its applicable
              liability limit, the umbrella may provide additional covered protection.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="scenario">
            <p class="scenario-label">Example: Dog-bite liability</p>
            <h4>A serious injury creates a large claim</h4>
            <p>
              A covered dog-bite claim requires surgery, rehabilitation, and time away
              from work. If the claim exceeds the underlying homeowners liability limit,
              the umbrella may help pay the remaining covered damages.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h3>Personal Injury Coverage</h3>
          <p>
            Personal injury coverage addresses certain covered non-physical offenses.
            It is different from bodily injury liability and may have its own selected
            limit.
          </p>

          <h4>Common Personal Injury Limits</h4>
          <ul class="limit-list">
            <li>$300,000</li>
            <li>$500,000</li>
            <li>$1 million</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            These options apply specifically to covered personal injury claims. The
            available offenses, limits, and relationship to the excess liability
            coverage depend on the insurer's policy form.
          </p>

          <div class="scenario">
            <p class="scenario-label">Example: Defamation</p>
            <h4>A false online statement causes harm</h4>
            <p>
              You publish false information about another person or business, and the
              statement causes measurable reputational or financial damage. If the
              allegations meet the policy's definition of a covered personal injury
              offense, the policy may respond subject to its terms.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="scenario">
            <p class="scenario-label">Example: Wrongful eviction</p>
            <h4>A landlord fails to follow the required process</h4>
            <p>
              A tenant alleges that they were removed improperly and files a lawsuit.
              If wrongful eviction is included within the policy's covered personal
              injury offenses, coverage may apply according to the policy language.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="scenario">
            <p class="scenario-label">Example: False arrest or imprisonment</p>
            <h4>A person alleges wrongful detention</h4>
            <p>
              A misunderstanding results in someone being detained based on allegations
              made against them. Coverage depends on the facts and whether the claim
              falls within a covered offense.
            </p>
          </div>

          <a class="next-link" href="#part-4">Continue to Part IV &rarr;</a>
        </section>

        <section class="part" id="part-4">
          <p class="part-number">Part IV</p>
          <h2>Exclusions, Cost, and Payment Options</h2>

          <h3>What Is Usually Not Covered?</h3>

          <div class="exclusion-list">
            <section>
              <h4>Your Own Injuries</h4>
              <p>
                Umbrella insurance protects against covered liability to others. It
                generally does not pay your own medical expenses.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h4>Damage to Your Own Property</h4>
              <p>
                It generally does not repair your home, vehicle, boat, or belongings.
                Those losses belong under the applicable property or physical-damage
                coverage when insured.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h4>Intentional or Criminal Acts</h4>
              <p>
                Deliberate injury, fraud, intentional property damage, and criminal
                misconduct are generally excluded.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h4>Business Activities</h4>
              <p>
                A personal umbrella is generally not a substitute for commercial
                liability or a commercial umbrella. Business exposures require
                appropriate business insurance unless specifically covered.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h4>Contractual Obligations</h4>
              <p>
                An umbrella generally does not pay an obligation merely because you
                accepted it through a contract. The policy language and nature of the
                obligation determine coverage.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h4>Uninsured or Ineligible Underlying Exposures</h4>
              <p>
                Umbrella insurers commonly require specified underlying policies and
                minimum liability limits. Failing to maintain them can create a gap or
                require you to absorb the amount that should have been carried beneath
                the umbrella.
              </p>
            </section>
          </div>

          <h3>How Much Does Umbrella Insurance Cost?</h3>
          <p>
            Premiums vary according to the insurer's underwriting and your overall
            liability exposure. Factors may include:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Driving history</li>
            <li>Number, age, and experience of household drivers</li>
            <li>Number of vehicles</li>
            <li>Homes and rental properties</li>
            <li>Boats, motorcycles, recreational vehicles, or other exposures</li>
            <li>Dogs or other animal-related exposures</li>
            <li>Prior claims</li>
            <li>Selected excess liability and personal injury limits</li>
            <li>Location and insurer-specific underwriting rules</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>Payment Options</h3>
          <p>
            Depending on the company, policyholders may be able to pay monthly,
            quarterly, semiannually, or annually.
          </p>

          <div class="price-note">
            <h4>Example pricing—not a universal quote</h4>
            <p>
              Depending on the carrier, selected limits, and individual risk,
              payments may range from around $70 to a couple hundred dollars per
              month, with annual premiums varying accordingly. Around $1,100 per year
              may be seen in some situations. Actual pricing must be determined by
              the insurer for the specific household and exposures.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The cost should never be separated from the coverage. A lower premium is
            only meaningful when you understand the limit, underlying requirements,
            covered exposures, exclusions, and how defense costs are handled.
          </p>

          <a class="next-link" href="#part-5">Continue to Part V &rarr;</a>
        </section>

        <section class="part" id="part-5">
          <p class="part-number">Part V</p>
          <h2>Who Should Consider Coverage and Common Questions</h2>

          <h3>Who Should Consider Umbrella Insurance?</h3>
          <p>
            Umbrella insurance is not only for exceptionally wealthy households.
            Anyone with assets, income, or future earnings may benefit from considering
            additional liability protection.
          </p>

          <div class="consider-grid">
            <section>
              <h4>Homeowners</h4>
              <p>Property ownership creates premises and personal liability exposure.</p>
            </section>
            <section>
              <h4>Landlords</h4>
              <p>Tenants, guests, contractors, and rental-property operations can create additional liability risks.</p>
            </section>
            <section>
              <h4>Families with Teenage Drivers</h4>
              <p>Less-experienced drivers can increase the household's automobile liability exposure.</p>
            </section>
            <section>
              <h4>People with Savings or Investments</h4>
              <p>A large covered judgment may threaten assets and future financial plans.</p>
            </section>
            <section>
              <h4>Professionals and Public-Facing Individuals</h4>
              <p>Income, visibility, and perceived resources can increase the consequences of a personal lawsuit.</p>
            </section>
            <section>
              <h4>Owners of Boats or Recreational Vehicles</h4>
              <p>Additional vehicles and activities can create additional opportunities for serious injury or damage.</p>
            </section>
            <section>
              <h4>Dog Owners</h4>
              <p>A severe animal-related injury can create substantial medical and legal damages when the exposure is eligible for coverage.</p>
            </section>
            <section>
              <h4>Anyone Seeking Greater Financial Protection</h4>
              <p>A household does not need millions in current assets to have income and a future worth protecting.</p>
            </section>
          </div>

          <h3>Frequently Asked Questions</h3>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details>
              <summary>Does umbrella insurance replace homeowners or auto insurance?</summary>
              <p>No. It works with qualifying underlying policies and generally responds after the applicable underlying liability limit is exhausted.</p>
            </details>

            <details>
              <summary>Does it cover damage to my own home or vehicle?</summary>
              <p>Generally no. Umbrella insurance is liability coverage, not first-party property coverage.</p>
            </details>

            <details>
              <summary>Does it pay my own medical bills?</summary>
              <p>Generally no. It is designed to protect against covered liability for injury or damage to others.</p>
            </details>

            <details>
              <summary>Are minimum underlying liability limits required?</summary>
              <p>Usually yes. The insurer will specify the required limits for auto, homeowners, and other underlying policies.</p>
            </details>

            <details>
              <summary>Does it cover legal defense costs?</summary>
              <p>Many policies provide a defense for covered claims, but how those costs are handled varies by policy form.</p>
            </details>

            <details>
              <summary>Can I purchase more than $5 million?</summary>
              <p>Some insurers offer higher limits, subject to eligibility and underwriting.</p>
            </details>

            <details>
              <summary>Is umbrella insurance expensive?</summary>
              <p>Pricing varies. Many households consider it an efficient way to add a substantial amount of liability protection, but the actual premium depends on the exposures and limits.</p>
            </details>

            <details>
              <summary>Is it worth it?</summary>
              <p>That depends on your assets, income, exposures, policy options, and comfort with financial risk. The decision should be based on your circumstances rather than a universal rule.</p>
            </details>
          </div>

          <a class="next-link" href="#part-6">Continue to Part VI &rarr;</a>
        </section>

        <section class="part" id="part-6">
          <p class="part-number">Part VI</p>
          <h2>Quick Reference and Final Thoughts</h2>

          <h3>Key Takeaways</h3>
          <ul class="checklist">
            <li>Umbrella insurance adds liability protection above qualifying underlying policies.</li>
            <li>It does not replace homeowners, auto, or other underlying insurance.</li>
            <li>Common excess liability options are $1 million through $5 million.</li>
            <li>Common personal injury options are $300,000, $500,000, and $1 million.</li>
            <li>Personal injury is different from bodily injury.</li>
            <li>Coverage, limits, exclusions, defense provisions, and requirements vary by insurer and state.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>Questions to Ask Your Insurance Professional</h3>
          <ul class="checklist">
            <li>What underlying liability limits must I maintain?</li>
            <li>Which excess liability limits are available?</li>
            <li>Does the policy include personal injury coverage?</li>
            <li>Which personal injury offenses are covered?</li>
            <li>What personal injury limits can I select?</li>
            <li>How are legal defense costs handled?</li>
            <li>Are all household drivers, homes, rentals, boats, and recreational vehicles eligible?</li>
            <li>Which exclusions apply to my specific exposures?</li>
            <li>Which payment plans are available?</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>Umbrella Insurance Quick Reference</h3>
          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Primary purpose</th>
